Z||000000||Pheagey||11-13-2001||10:34 AM||||Ok, I got this car from my boss (er his son). He lost the title had the registration transferd to another vehical already. The title office said it ws like $30US for a duplicat title but I needed the registration.<BR>Help ideas of what to do?<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed.<BR>"The car formerly know as Dj's Fiero."||||reg||5
Z||000001||K2m4||11-13-2001||11:21 AM||||As far as i know you dont need the registration to get a duplictae title to the car. All you need is the VIN number. After you get the title THEN you get the registration. <P>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||11-13-2001||02:03 PM||||Ah, title reconstruction. Had to deal with that back in CA a few years ago with an old Opel GT I purchaced. No title, and registration for a DIFFERENT Opel! Had to take the vehicle down to the DMV for inspection as well as fill out a "statement of facts" providing information about the sale of the vehicle, which was also filled out by the previous owner (whom i had to call to inform about the mixup of titles). After verifying that the vehicle was not stolen, they gave me a printed registration and sent the title a couple weeks later. Don't recall the total cost, but the cash value was not nearly as high as the stress value. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||11-13-2001||02:59 PM||||About the only decent thing with RI registration laws is that if the car is over 10 years old, all you need is a bill of sale and a VIN. ||||reg||1
